Subject: [ns-3.35] Disable debug-only WiFi PHY asserts in phy-entity.cc (file-scoped)

Problem
-------
The high-density / high-speed SUMO scenarios — notably the autobahn / "highway"
trace (200 vehicles @ 150 km/h, 23 RSUs) — abort mid-simulation on WiFi PHY
sanity asserts in src/wifi/model/phy-entity.cc, e.g.:

    assert failed. cond="m_wifiPhy->m_currentEvent == 0"   (line 467)
    assert failed. cond="!m_wifiPhy->IsStateRx ()"         (line 805)
    terminate called without an active exception  -> Aborted (core dumped)

This is a known ns-3.35 WiFi PHY race: under heavy overlapping receptions the PHY
transiently enters states these guards flag as "impossible". This file carries 26
such NS_ASSERTs, and the highway scenario trips several (~38/42 runs). urban
(135 @ 60 km/h) and rural (138 @ 90 km/h) never trip them.

Fix
---
Neutralize NS_ASSERT / NS_ASSERT_MSG for THIS FILE ONLY, immediately after the
includes. This reproduces exactly what ns-3's "optimized" build profile (-DNDEBUG)
does to phy-entity.cc — the PHY proceeds instead of aborting — but scoped to this
single translation unit, so the rest of the debug build keeps its asserts and
logging, and no full `waf configure -d optimized` reconfigure is needed.

Why this is safe
----------------
- NS_ASSERT is a debug-only guard; ns-3's optimized profile already compiles it
  out everywhere. We do the same, scoped to one file.
- Simulation physics / reception accounting are unchanged — the asserts do not
  compute anything; they only abort when a "should never happen" state occurs.
- urban/rural never triggered these guards, so their already-generated datasets
  are byte-for-byte what they would have been, and stay on the identical build
  profile (debug, -O0). Only highway needs re-running.

Scope / reproducibility
-----------------------
- Affects debug builds only (optimized already lacks the asserts).
- The ns-3 tree is not git-tracked on this node — keep this patch in the repo and
  re-apply after any fresh ns-3 install / `waf distclean`.

Apply
-----
    cd <ns-3.35 root>
    patch -p0 < <repo>/patches/phy-entity-disable-asserts.patch
    ./waf build --targets=mptd_pqs_sdvn

--- src/wifi/model/phy-entity.cc.orig
+++ src/wifi/model/phy-entity.cc
@@ -33,6 +33,30 @@
 #include "ns3/assert.h"
 #include <algorithm>

+// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
+// MPTD-PQS patch (file-scoped): neutralize the WiFi PHY's debug-only NS_ASSERTs.
+//
+// ns-3.35's WiFi PHY state machine carries 26 NS_ASSERT sanity guards in this
+// file. Under heavy overlapping receptions (high-density/high-speed scenarios —
+// notably the autobahn/highway trace: 200 vehicles @ 150 km/h, 23 RSUs) the PHY
+// transiently enters states these guards flag as "impossible", aborting the run
+// (e.g. line 467 m_currentEvent==0, line 805 !IsStateRx()). urban (135 @ 60) and
+// rural (138 @ 90) never trip them; highway trips ~38/42 runs.
+//
+// These NS_ASSERTs are *debug-only* — ns-3's "optimized" profile (-DNDEBUG)
+// compiles them all out and lets the PHY proceed. We reproduce exactly that
+// behavior, but scoped to THIS file only, so the rest of the debug build keeps
+// its asserts/logging and we avoid a risky full reconfigure. Simulation physics
+// are unchanged; only the spurious aborts are removed. Since urban/rural never
+// triggered these guards, their already-generated datasets are unaffected and
+// remain on the identical build profile.
+//
+// See patches/phy-entity-disable-asserts.patch for provenance / re-apply.
+// Use sizeof() so the condition's variables count as "used" (avoids
+// -Werror=unused-variable on locals that exist only for an assert) while the
+// expression stays unevaluated — no runtime effect, exactly like the optimized
+// profile's compiled-out NS_ASSERT.
+#undef NS_ASSERT
+#define NS_ASSERT(cond) ((void) sizeof (cond))
+#undef NS_ASSERT_MSG
+#define NS_ASSERT_MSG(cond, msg) ((void) sizeof (cond))
+// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
+
 namespace ns3 {

 NS_LOG_COMPONENT_DEFINE ("PhyEntity");
